[D-BUS Service]
Name=com.arkmicro.auto
# 2026-07-28: redirect sink's own stdout/stderr so its prints --
# including AlsaHandle::play()'s "play:225" XRUN-recovery line, the
# actual mechanism behind the choppy-audio investigation -- are
# visible for correlation instead of being lost.
#
# 2026-08-02: switched from /dev/kmsg to a real file
# (/data/sink_output.log). /dev/kmsg is both rate-limited and
# buffer-bounded by the kernel -- under a burst of output (exactly
# what happens during an XRUN storm, dozens of play:225 lines in under
# a second) it can silently drop lines, which is effectively lossy in
# precisely the situation this logging exists to capture. A plain file
# has neither limit. glibc still fully-buffers stdout when it isn't a
# TTY (no stdbuf/script applet in this busybox build to force
# line-buffering), so lines may still arrive in bursts rather than
# immediately -- acceptable for post-hoc correlation against dmesg
# timestamps, not real-time monitoring. `>` (not `>>`) so each session
# starts a fresh log rather than accumulating indefinitely across
# reboots.
#
# 2026-08-02: prefixed with `chrt -f 50` -- real-time (SCHED_FIFO)
# scheduling priority for sink and every thread it spawns. This is a
# deliberate deviation from stock, not a restoration of stock behavior
# -- checked the real stock rootfs dump's own com.arkmicro.auto.service
# directly, it's a bare `Exec=/usr/bin/sink` with no priority wrapping
# at all, and sink's own binary (same Holden-sourced binary on both
# stock and here) never calls sched_setscheduler/pthread_setschedparam
# internally either. This is a practical mitigation attempt for the
# long-running AA media audio stutter investigation
# (docs/AUDIO_SUBSYSTEM_INVESTIGATION.md) after live tracing (strace)
# proved too invasive on this hardware to safely gather more diagnostic
# data -- not a fix for a specific confirmed mechanism, a scheduling-
# priority experiment covering several of the remaining open theories
# (single-core CPU contention, IRQ-disable windows, generic scheduling
# starvation) at once regardless of which one is real. Threads created
# via pthread_create() after this inherit the parent's SCHED_FIFO
# policy/priority by default, so this covers every thread sink spawns,
# not just its main one, without needing to enumerate/attach to
# individual thread IDs after the fact. Revert this line alone (back to
# the previous Exec=) if it doesn't help or causes new problems --
# genuine SCHED_FIFO on a single-core system that never yields can
# starve *other* processes if sink ever spins instead of blocking.
Exec=/bin/sh -c "exec chrt -f 50 /usr/bin/sink > /data/sink_output.log 2>&1"
